  "worked_solution": "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)(x+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=\\pm 1$ or $x=0.$ Since $0\\in [-1,1],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,0]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [0,1]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
